<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix S1 </span>Analogical Particle System</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We simulated <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1024</annotation></semantics></math> point particles in a two–dimensional periodic square of side <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\sqrt{N/\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\sqrt{N/\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> at number density <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.8</annotation></semantics></math> (dimensionless units). For equilibrium assemblies (Lennard–Jones, Morse, SALR) we integrated overdamped Langevin dynamics with Euler–Maruyama,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\mathbf{r}_{i}=\mu\!\sum_{j\neq i}\mathbf{F}_{ij}\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2\,T(t)}\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{W}_{i},\qquad\mathbf{F}_{ij}=-\nabla U(r_{ij})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.600em;" width="0.600em"><mi>μ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\mathbf{r}_{i}=\mu\!\sum_{j\neq i}\mathbf{F}_{ij}\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2\,T(t)}\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{W}_{i},\qquad\mathbf{F}_{ij}=-\nabla U(r_{ij}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">using the minimum–image convention and a cell–linked neighbor list (rebuilt every 10 steps). We used <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1</annotation></semantics></math>, time step <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an annealing schedule</p>
<table id="A1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(t)=T_{\min}+\bigl(T_{0}-T_{\min}\bigr)\!\left(1-\tfrac{t}{S-1}\right)^{p},\quad(T_{0}=0.4,\;T_{\min}=0.02,\;p=1.5,\;S=3000)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(t)=T_{\min}+\bigl(T_{0}-T_{\min}\bigr)\!\left(1-\tfrac{t}{S-1}\right)^{p},\quad(T_{0}=0.4,\;T_{\min}=0.02,\;p=1.5,\;S=3000).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Potentials (truncated at <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> without shifting) were</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathrm{LJ}}(r)=4\varepsilon\!\left[\left(\tfrac{\sigma}{r}\right)^{12}-\left(\tfrac{\sigma}{r}\right)^{6}\right],\quad\sigma=1,\;\varepsilon=1,\;r_{\mathrm{c}}=2.5\sigma," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>LJ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.296em;" width="0.296em"><mi>ε</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>12</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathrm{LJ}}(r)=4\varepsilon\!\left[\left(\tfrac{\sigma}{r}\right)^{12}-\left(\tfrac{\sigma}{r}\right)^{6}\right],\quad\sigma=1,\;\varepsilon=1,\;r_{\mathrm{c}}=2.5\sigma,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathrm{Morse}}(r)=D_{e}\!\left(1-e^{-\alpha(r-r_{e})}\right)^{2}-D_{e},\quad D_{e}=1,\;\alpha=3,\;r_{e}=1.1,\;r_{\mathrm{c}}=3.0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>Morse</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathrm{Morse}}(r)=D_{e}\!\left(1-e^{-\alpha(r-r_{e})}\right)^{2}-D_{e},\quad D_{e}=1,\;\alpha=3,\;r_{e}=1.1,\;r_{\mathrm{c}}=3.0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathrm{SALR}}(r)=-A\,e^{-(r/\sigma_{a})^{2}}+B\,e^{-(r/\sigma_{r})^{2}},\quad A=1,\;\sigma_{a}=0.8,\;B=0.30,\;\sigma_{r}=3.0,\;r_{\mathrm{c}}=4.0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>SALR</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.30</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathrm{SALR}}(r)=-A\,e^{-(r/\sigma_{a})^{2}}+B\,e^{-(r/\sigma_{r})^{2}},\quad A=1,\;\sigma_{a}=0.8,\;B=0.30,\;\sigma_{r}=3.0,\;r_{\mathrm{c}}=4.0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Active flocking (Vicsek) used headings <math id="A1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> updated by the mean neighbor orientation within radius <math id="A1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{v}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{v}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> plus uniform angular noise <math id="A1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\xi_{i}\!\sim\!\mathcal{U}[-\eta/2,\eta/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}\!\sim\!\mathcal{U}[-\eta/2,\eta/2]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0.25</annotation></semantics></math>, then self–propelled motion <math id="A1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{r}_{i}+v_{0}(\cos\theta_{i},\sin\theta_{i})\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{r}_{i}+v_{0}(\cos\theta_{i},\sin\theta_{i})\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}=0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}=0.03</annotation></semantics></math>. We ran <math id="A1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=2000</annotation></semantics></math> steps for the Vicsek model and enforced periodic boundaries. See Figure <a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure S1 ‣ Appendix S1 Analogical Particle System ‣ MusicSwarm: Biologically Inspired Intelligence for Music Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a> for a visualization of the interactions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11973v1/Fig_2002.png" id="A1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/212;" width="476" height="212" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S1: </span>Local interaction rules used in the analogy.
A: Pair potentials for equilibrium systems: Lennard–Jones (short-range repulsion with a single cohesive well) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, Morse (tunable well depth and range, controlling effective bond elasticity) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, and SALR (short-range attraction plus long-range repulsion, leading to microphase clustering) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
B: Alignment interactions in the Vicsek active-matter model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>: top, neighborhood weighting function with cutoff radius <math id="A1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>; bottom, angular alignment preference modeled by a cosine kernel.
Together, these four interaction laws span canonical cases of conservative equilibrium potentials and nonequilibrium alignment rules, forming the basis of our particle-system analogy for multi-agent interactions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Visualizations show final configurations colored by local order: for LJ and Morse we computed the hexatic parameter</p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{6}(i)=\frac{1}{n_{i}}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}e^{\,6\mathrm{i}\theta_{ij}},\qquad|\psi_{6}|\in[0,1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn> 6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{6}(i)=\frac{1}{n_{i}}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}}e^{\,6\mathrm{i}\theta_{ij}},\qquad|\psi_{6}|\in[0,1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">using neighbors within <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1.5\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1.5\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>; for SALR we used normalized neighbor counts within <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1.25\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.25</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1.25\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as a local–density proxy; for Vicsek we colored by normalized heading <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta/(2\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta/(2\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>. The radial distribution function <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(r)</annotation></semantics></math> was computed from pair distances (to <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\max}=L/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\max}=L/2</annotation></semantics></math>) via a histogram with bin width <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta r</annotation></semantics></math> and normalized as</p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(r)=\frac{H(r)}{N\,2\pi r\,\Delta r\,\rho}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(r)=\frac{H(r)}{N\,2\pi r\,\Delta r\,\rho},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(r)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of pairs in the shell <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[r,r+\Delta r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[r,r+\Delta r)</annotation></semantics></math>. Random initial positions were uniform in the box (seed <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=42</annotation></semantics></math>). Figures were rendered with scatter markers and per–panel colorbars; <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(r)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(r)=1</annotation></semantics></math> is indicated by a dashed baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This experiment was designed to isolate the role of local interactions in producing qualitatively different global organizations under controlled conditions. We fixed particle number (<math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1024</annotation></semantics></math>), density, integration scheme, and annealing schedule, so that the only salient difference across panels is the interaction law. The three equilibrium pair potentials span canonical cases: Lennard–Jones (short-range repulsion with a single cohesive well) yields dense crystals and serves as a baseline for close-packed order <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>; Morse provides a tunable well depth and range, capturing stiffer/softer “bond elasticity” and shifting crystallinity vs. fluidity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>; and SALR (short-range attraction, long-range repulsion) represents *competing* interactions that generically drive microphase separation (clusters/stripes) rather than bulk phase separation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. To contrast equilibrium self-assembly with nonequilibrium patterning, we also include the Vicsek active-matter model, where alignment interactions plus noise generate flocking bands and polar order that are not captured by isotropic conservative forces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Readouts are chosen to be interaction-agnostic: configurations (geometry), and <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(r)</annotation></semantics></math> (two-point structure), so differences in the figure can be directly attributed to the potentials rather than to protocol or visualization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11973v1/Fig_2001.png" id="A1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/281;" width="381" height="281" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S2: </span>Radial distribution functions (RDFs) <math id="A1.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(r)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1024</annotation></semantics></math> under different local interaction rules.
Top left (Lennard–Jones): nearly flat profile close to unity, indicating the absence of long-range crystalline order <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
Top right (Morse): sharp peaks at regular intervals reflecting strong crystallinity and long-range correlations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
Bottom left (SALR): a pronounced first peak followed by damped oscillations and a split secondary maximum, characteristic of cluster-forming microphase order <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
Bottom right (Vicsek): broad decaying correlations without oscillatory structure, consistent with transient flocking clusters and the lack of equilibrium crystalline order <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix S2 </span>Equilibrium analysis</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S2.1 </span>Mean step-to-step trait change</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> index agents, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> index traits, and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math> index iterations.
Denote the (normalized) trait value of agent <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> on trait <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> at iteration <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i,k}^{(t)}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i,k}^{(t)}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each step <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to t{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to t{+}1</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the <em id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mean absolute change</em> across all agents and traits:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Delta}_{t}~=~\frac{1}{M_{t}}\sum_{(i,k)\in\Omega_{t}}\bigl|\,x_{i,k}^{(t+1)}-x_{i,k}^{(t)}\,\bigr|,\qquad\Omega_{t}=\{(i,k):x_{i,k}^{(t)}\text{ and }x_{i,k}^{(t+1)}\text{ observed}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.608em" rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> observed</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Delta}_{t}~=~\frac{1}{M_{t}}\sum_{(i,k)\in\Omega_{t}}\bigl|\,x_{i,k}^{(t+1)}-x_{i,k}^{(t)}\,\bigr|,\qquad\Omega_{t}=\{(i,k):x_{i,k}^{(t)}\text{ and }x_{i,k}^{(t+1)}\text{ observed}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{t}=|\Omega_{t}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{t}=|\Omega_{t}|</annotation></semantics></math>. (Entries missing at either step are omitted.)</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We plot <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Delta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Delta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> (the “next” iteration). Small values indicate that, on average,
agents would <em id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">barely change</em> their traits on the next round; sustained lows (plateaus) and local minima of
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Delta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Delta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> therefore mark <em id="A2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">low-motion basins</em> or <em id="A2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local rest points</em> in the dynamics.
In our experiments, early dips (e.g., <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{+}1\approx 4\text{\textendash}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{+}1\approx 4\text{\textendash}5</annotation></semantics></math>) show transient stabilization, while later,
deeper dips (e.g., <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{+}1\approx 9\text{\textendash}11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{+}1\approx 9\text{\textendash}11</annotation></semantics></math>) indicate convergence to a more stable configuration.
This is consistent with a behavioral fixed-point interpretation (<em id="A2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no unilateral change</em> at the profile),
though a payoff-based Nash certificate requires explicit utilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S2.2 </span>Learned dynamics</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze a swarm of <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> agents with <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> normalized traits. Let <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(t)}_{i,k}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(t)}_{i,k}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote trait <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of agent <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at iteration <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">observed mean step change</em> is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\Delta}_{t}\;=\;\frac{1}{M_{t}}\sum_{(i,k)\in\Omega_{t}}\left|x^{(t+1)}_{i,k}-x^{(t)}_{i,k}\right|,\qquad\Omega_{t}=\{(i,k):x^{(t)}_{i,k},x^{(t+1)}_{i,k}\ \text{observed}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext>observed</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Delta}_{t}\;=\;\frac{1}{M_{t}}\sum_{(i,k)\in\Omega_{t}}\left|x^{(t+1)}_{i,k}-x^{(t)}_{i,k}\right|,\qquad\Omega_{t}=\{(i,k):x^{(t)}_{i,k},x^{(t+1)}_{i,k}\ \text{observed}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which we plot against <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> (missing entries are omitted).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain a simple <em id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learned best–response</em> dynamics, for each trait <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> we pool all agents and all consecutive saved iterations <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\!\to\!t{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\!\to\!t{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> and fit the linear regression</p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(t+1)}_{i,k}~=~\alpha_{k}\,x^{(t)}_{i,k}\;+\;\beta_{k}\,\overline{x}^{(t)}_{N(i),k}\;+\;\gamma_{k}\;+\;\varepsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.608em" rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(t+1)}_{i,k}~=~\alpha_{k}\,x^{(t)}_{i,k}\;+\;\beta_{k}\,\overline{x}^{(t)}_{N(i),k}\;+\;\gamma_{k}\;+\;\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the interaction graph is a <span id="A2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">line (path)</span>: interior agents have <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(i)=\{i{-}1,i{+}1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(i)=\{i{-}1,i{+}1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the two ends have a single neighbor. Here <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{x}^{(t)}_{N(i),k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{x}^{(t)}_{N(i),k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the average over <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(i)</annotation></semantics></math>. Ordinary least squares yields <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k},\gamma_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha_{k},\beta_{k},\gamma_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and induces, for each trait, a linear map on the full agent vector</p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x^{(t+1)}_{:,k}~=~M_{k}\,x^{(t)}_{:,k}+c_{k},\qquad M_{k}=\alpha_{k}I+\beta_{k}P,\ \ c_{k}=\gamma_{k}\mathbf{1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(t+1)}_{:,k}~=~M_{k}\,x^{(t)}_{:,k}+c_{k},\qquad M_{k}=\alpha_{k}I+\beta_{k}P,\ \ c_{k}=\gamma_{k}\mathbf{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> the path (line) adjacency, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\mathrm{diag}(A\mathbf{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\mathrm{diag}(A\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=D^{-1}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=D^{-1}A</annotation></semantics></math> the row–normalized adjacency (ends have degree 1, interiors degree 2). Iterating this map from the first observed profile produces a <em id="A2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">model–predicted</em> step–change curve</p>
<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\Delta}^{\text{model}}_{t}\;=\;\frac{1}{NK}\sum_{i,k}\left|\,\bigl(M_{k}x^{(t)}_{:,k}+c_{k}\bigr)_{i}-x^{(t)}_{i,k}\,\right|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em">|</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Delta}^{\text{model}}_{t}\;=\;\frac{1}{NK}\sum_{i,k}\left|\,\bigl(M_{k}x^{(t)}_{:,k}+c_{k}\bigr)_{i}-x^{(t)}_{i,k}\,\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">To compare fairly with the empirical process (which includes exploration noise, clipping, and asynchrony), we align the model to data via an affine calibration that estimates a timescale factor <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and a noise floor <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda,\delta)\;=\;\arg\min_{\lambda,\delta}\sum_{t}\!\Bigl(\overline{\Delta}_{t}-\bigl(\delta+\lambda\,\overline{\Delta}^{\text{model}}_{t}\bigr)\Bigr)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda,\delta)\;=\;\arg\min_{\lambda,\delta}\sum_{t}\!\Bigl(\overline{\Delta}_{t}-\bigl(\delta+\lambda\,\overline{\Delta}^{\text{model}}_{t}\bigr)\Bigr)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">and we plot the calibrated overlay <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta+\lambda\,\overline{\Delta}^{\text{model}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta+\lambda\,\overline{\Delta}^{\text{model}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Empirically, the calibrated model tracks the decay and plateau in <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Delta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Delta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> capturing the persistent motion floor, supporting practical convergence to a stable behavioral fixed point.
Parameters are listed in Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table S1 ‣ S2.2 Learned dynamics ‣ Appendix S2 Equilibrium analysis ‣ MusicSwarm: Biologically Inspired Intelligence for Music Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a> and <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table S2 ‣ S2.2 Learned dynamics ‣ Appendix S2 Equilibrium analysis ‣ MusicSwarm: Biologically Inspired Intelligence for Music Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a>. Figure <a href="#A2.F3" title="Figure S3 ‣ S2.2 Learned dynamics ‣ Appendix S2 Equilibrium analysis ‣ MusicSwarm: Biologically Inspired Intelligence for Music Composition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a> shows the fixed-point residual heatmap at the terminal iteration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table S1: </span>Per–trait best–response coefficients fitted by pooled OLS on the line topology. <math id="A2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the in–sample coefficient of determination; <math id="A2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of transitions used.</figcaption>
<table id="A2.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
